WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
Today, AI can write everything for you.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.520
That's why you sound exactly like

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.160
everyone else. And standing apart from

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.840
others is one of the most valuable

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.840
things you can do for your career, your

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.600
relationships, and your future. So, how

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:23.760
do you write absurdly well with AI but

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:26.440
stop sounding like ChatGPT and still

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.040
write like a human being? I will share a

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:32.800
five-step AI writing system that will

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:37.160
make everything you write clearer, more

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.920
insightful, and unmistakably yours. So,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:45.440
let's start with the story that changed

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:47.280
AI forever. Move 37. It was March 2016,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.240
and that was the day when one of the

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.840
strongest human players of a board game

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.560
called Go was playing against AI. Lee

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.800
Sedol had held the world championship

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:03.760
titles maybe 18 times. And Go is this

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.200
3,000-year-old game. It looks very

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:11.960
simple. Black stones, white stones,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.640
uh 19 by 19 board, but there are roughly

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.080
10 to the 170

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.280
legal board positions.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.080
That's more than the number of atoms in

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.280
the observable universe. Millions of

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:29.040
people were watching, and midway through

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.440
the game at the 37th move, Lee walked

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.320
out for a break. He wanted to smoke a

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.520
cigarette and come back.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.960
And when he came back, he looked at the

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.880
board,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.440
and he couldn't believe it.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:46.640
AlphaGo had just played a move that

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:48.520
looked like complete heresy. That was

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.600
move 37.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:55.040
And 100 [clears throat] moves later, AI

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.320
won the game. So, AI can do all of that.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:01.440
Then, why does AI that we use today

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:04.360
write in such a predictable generic

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.120
fashion? When we understand why that

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.000
happens, we can become better writers,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.920
and more importantly, we can become

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:16.760
better thinkers. The AI that we use

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:21.280
daily, like Claude or Gemini or ChatGPT,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:24.000
are called large language models or LLM.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.959
Now, those are built very differently

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.320
than AlphaGo. So, there's a big gap

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:33.520
between what AI generates and what you

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.959
should generate. And that gap creates

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.920
five problems in the way we all

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:41.640
communicate. So, let's cover those five

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.080
pitfalls next, and then we'll come up

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.040
with a five-step system that helps you

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.880
navigate around those five problems.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.280
Problem number one.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.400
Confidently drunk.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:58.640
In May of 2024,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:02.440
uh user asked Google, search how to make

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.800
sure that cheese stays stuck on a pizza.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:09.720
Now, Google's AI overview suggested

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.760
adding non-toxic glue to the sauce to

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.560
make [music] it stickier.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.920
It's true story.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.680
Now, AI understood words from the query.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.840
It understood [music]

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:24.720
pizza, cheese, sticking, and then it

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.760
found a joke from Reddit and thought

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.280
that was the solution.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.600
AI hallucinates, and it is so confident

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.920
while it's doing it. It's like having a

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.560
conversation with Albert Einstein

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.320
when he's drunk.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.360
He's smart, but he will just make things

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.000
up,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.480
and he won't know that he's doing it.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.519
So, that's problem number one. The

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:53.360
second problem, a broken record.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:56.400
AI learns patterns from the internet.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.880
Right? So, it predicts and remixes those

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.400
patterns all the time. So, it creates

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.760
structures that are familiar and

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.720
comfortable. And it's stuck doing that

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.640
because, well, that's the key feature.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:13.720
Problem number three, smart and empty.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:16.600
AI is extremely good at producing lines

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.000
in a shape of an insight.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:23.240
For instance, love without [snorts]

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.800
structure is a liability.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.919
I don't know what that means.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.400
Again,

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:31.480
words are fine, and it sounds profound,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.240
and it's pretty smart and deep, or at

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.720
least it sounds like that. But, what did

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.320
that actually mean? Because the moment

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.800
you take a closer look at it, it all

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:44.720
falls apart. Problem number four,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:48.160
clarity lost in noise.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.440
You ask AI to say something simple,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:55.200
and it might come back with some

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.640
abstraction or jargon. For instance, it

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.120
will happily create this text.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:03.400
Professional growth requires the

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.680
intentional strategy and development of

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:09.880
capabilities that align with emerging

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:12.200
opportunities across an increasingly

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.040
dynamic career landscape.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.023
That's [clears throat] a mouthful.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.680
And there is a real idea somewhere in

00:05:17.023 --> 00:05:23.080
[music] there, but it gets lost in these

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:26.040
abstractions that AI generates. If you

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:27.160
read it again, the idea is short and

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.880
simple.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:33.720
Learn things people need.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.480
That's it. Problem number five, AI is

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.760
boring.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:40.680
AI can give you a perfectly good line,

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.320
but it won't give you any reason to read

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.720
the next one. [music] AI writing has no

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:50.200
tension in it, no intrigue.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.048
So, it's all predictable and mostly

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.200
boring. So, those are the five problems.

00:05:53.048 --> 00:05:55.920
>> [music]

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.520
>> Of course, the solution is not to stop

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.920
using AI.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:03.240
That's inevitable nowadays. But, you got

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.960
to use it in a way that eliminates these

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:08.600
shortcomings. That [music] requires a

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:12.040
framework. So, let's walk through the

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:15.400
five steps of that framework. I'll also

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:17.120
share some example prompts you can use

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.120
for each one of them or at least four of

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.720
them.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:24.400
Together, they spell the thing you're

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.720
trying to create, your unique voice. AI

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.120
gives you an accent, and here's how you

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.360
get your voice back. So, let's go to the

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.919
first one.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.640
V is for verified.

00:06:32.919 --> 00:06:37.000
Here, [snorts]

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:38.640
you're fighting the AI hallucinations

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.440
and asking,

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.560
"Is this real?"

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.160
In 2023,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:49.200
a lawyer named Steven Schwartz used

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:53.120
ChatGPT to research a legal brief. And

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:56.840
of course, ChatGPT gave him cases and

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.840
citations and quotations and judicial

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:02.120
opinions, but there was only one

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:04.400
problem. None of those cases existed.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.560
When the opposing party's lawyers

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:10.440
couldn't find these cases in the real

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.960
world, Schwartz went back to ChatGPT and

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:16.320
asked if these cases were real.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:19.200
ChatGPT assured him they were.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.720
So, it's like hallucinations on top of

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.200
hallucinations.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.600
And of course, this is a 3-year-old

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:29.520
story, and AI has gotten much better.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:32.680
But, when it comes to numbers and names

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.960
and data and dates and research, my

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:39.040
first instinct still is to verify what

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:43.160
AI generates. I look for links. I look

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:46.240
for citations. And I ask AI to verify

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:49.080
any claim. And the move here is to treat

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.960
AI like a researcher

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.200
whose work requires a supervising

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.840
editor, you.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:08:00.000
So, for example,

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:02.320
here's a prompt. You can give it a draft

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.040
that you're writing and ask AI to do the

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:10.600
following. Extract every factual claim,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:13.360
statistic, data, date, facts, quotes

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:18.160
from this draft. Mark each of those

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:21.200
items verified, unverified, or disputed.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.960
Go out on the internet and find exact

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.480
links and citations that verify each

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:28.840
claim. Give me the strongest primary

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.520
source for each. Give me a list of

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:35.080
claims and their verification links and

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:37.960
citations in a bullet form. That's it.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:40.719
And on all three platforms,

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:43.960
there are two tricks. One, you can turn

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:47.360
the deep research mode on.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:50.880
And AI will give you a plan of how it's

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:54.000
going to go out on the internet and what

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:57.640
sources will it research. And the second

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:01.280
trick, cross-verify. Ask Claude to

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:04.320
verify Gemini's work and Gemini to

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:06.520
verify ChatGPT's work and so on. And by

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.920
the way, I talk about these frameworks

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:11.160
and systems and go deeper in my weekly

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.000
newsletter.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.560
It's every Tuesday morning. Please

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.480
subscribe. I love writing it and it's

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.880
totally free. And now let's go to the

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:25.720
second step. O is for owned. In a

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:29.800
Cornell University researchers ran an

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:34.040
interesting experiment with 118 people,

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:37.716
60 in India and 58 in the US.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.880
Each person had four writing prompts.

00:09:37.716 --> 00:09:40.480
>> [music]

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:42.956
>> Your favorite food, your favorite public

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.960
figure, your favorite holidays, and so

00:09:42.956 --> 00:09:46.360
[music] on.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:49.320
Half of them were asked to type it

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:52.920
themselves. The other half typed with

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.240
the assistance of AI suggestions.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.520
And by the end, [music]

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.120
Indians writing with AI were producing

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:02.680
more Americanized essays [music]

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:06.080
than Indians writing on their own.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.600
Indians who used AI, their favorite food

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.480
was pizza. [music]

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:12.760
Their favorite public figure starting

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.480
with S was Sylvester Stallone, and not

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:18.680
Shah Rukh Khan. Their favorite holiday

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.080
was [music] Christmas. Now, nobody made

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.080
them do it.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:24.400
But,

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:27.000
AI made all of it homogenized, and

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.152
generic, and ordinary.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.280
The familiar.

00:10:28.152 --> 00:10:31.280
>> [music]

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:34.160
>> The writers lost their cultural nuance.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:36.760
So, the key question here is does your

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.040
writing, or any kind of communication,

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.160
have your fingerprints, your unique

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.720
touch?

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:45.680
What makes you extraordinary?

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:48.320
The right move here is to give AI the

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:50.920
raw material from your life, because

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.640
that's your fingerprint. Something that

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.972
others won't have. So, a prompt would be

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.160
like, "Do not write anything yet.

00:10:56.972 --> 00:11:00.480
>> [music]

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:03.360
>> Interview me about this topic. Ask me

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:07.000
one question at a time about what I have

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.120
personally seen, or done, or believed,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.120
or learned, [music]

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:14.720
or got wrong. Push back whenever my

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:17.960
answer is vague. Stop only when you have

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:21.280
five specific details that are relevant

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:23.839
to the topic, and have come from me."

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:26.280
So, that's [music] all for owned. Now,

00:11:23.839 --> 00:11:30.200
let's go to the third step, which is I

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:32.480
for insightful. Is there actually a so

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:34.400
what behind your idea? That's the

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:37.600
question you should be asking. Let's say

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:39.760
you're making a LinkedIn post and AI

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:42.360
comes up with something like "High

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.120
performers succeed because they extract

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:47.920
wisdom from failure." Okay, it sounds

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.920
fine, but you should ask

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.400
"Who in the world is arguing that

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:55.800
successful people learn nothing from

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:59.160
failure?"

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:01.920
No one. There's no unique point of view.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:03.840
Everybody knows that failure matters and

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.000
it teaches you lessons. So what do you

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:09.960
do? There are three quick moves that can

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:12.680
get you out of this mess. First, bring

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:15.480
your own experience. For instance, you

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:18.920
could have said "Last week I got hired

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:21.800
after 4 months of grueling job search."

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:24.840
Those 4 months taught me things that

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:27.440
were more valuable than actually getting

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:30.200
the offer letter. It's the same message,

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:33.040
but there's you in it. There's something

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:36.640
unique. Another way to bring something

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:39.839
insightful is to go deeper but inside.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:43.680
Before I write anything, I often force

00:12:39.839 --> 00:12:48.080
myself to finish three sentences.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:49.640
One, most people believe in XYZ and

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:53.040
whatever it is.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:56.120
I believe in ABC.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:59.200
And the difference is 1 2 3.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:02.320
Now, you don't always have to look for

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:05.280
the counterintuitive insight. Until you

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:07.839
find that unique angle,

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.680
you probably have a topic,

00:13:07.839 --> 00:13:14.200
but you still don't have an insight. And

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:17.760
the third move here is to use AI as your

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:21.160
opponent. Lots of people use AI to

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:24.680
manufacture an insight, but you should

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:27.000
give it your insight and then ask AI to

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:30.960
attack it. You could prompt AI like

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:32.640
this, "Act as an intelligent skeptic.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.760
Here's the point I'm trying to make."

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.720
And you put your point there.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:40.520
"What would you disagree with and why?

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:43.720
Attack my point of view by asking, 'So

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:46.800
what?' until you're satisfied."

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:48.080
All right. So, the next one is C for

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:51.040
clear.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:53.400
Will they understand what you're saying?

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:56.720
That's the question to ask. Many years

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:59.560
ago, I was at a company where I was

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:02.440
reporting to the CEO.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:05.240
And he and I were sitting there during

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:08.480
my um annual review.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:11.160
And he was such a thoughtful leader with

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:13.640
a very high EQ.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.800
He had a PhD in economics, brilliant

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:20.320
brilliant guy. And he gave me the most

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:21.920
useful feedback I've ever received. He

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:23.240
said,

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.120
"Sandeep,

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:29.440
you have this habit of hiding behind

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:31.040
your humor.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:35.400
You deflect.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:38.680
You use sarcasm instead of candor.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:40.280
It's going to make people unsure of who

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:45.280
you are

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:47.680
and whether they can trust you."

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:49.500
E- Even right now, as I remember that

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:49.680
moment, I

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:52.040
>> [music]

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.080
>> I mean, I can feel the weight of those

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:56.120
words

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:57.080
because they hit me like a ton of

00:14:56.120 --> 00:15:00.000
bricks.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:02.680
I had no idea. I thought I was being

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.920
warm and and funny and

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.080
non-confrontational,

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:09.480
but

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:12.680
what was being seen by others was

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:15.360
completely different. You're the last

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:17.320
one who can judge your own mode of

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:19.560
communication and how it gets

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.285
interpreted by others is not in your

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:20.600
control.

00:15:20.285 --> 00:15:22.960
>> [music]

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:26.600
>> So, if you're trying to create more

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:28.360
clarity in today's noise-infested world,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:31.760
here [music] are the three moves you can

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:33.440
make. First, try to say it out loud when

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:36.160
you trying to write.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:38.960
Speaking is faster than writing, so

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:41.200
you'll reach for simpler words that you

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.640
know instead of the words that sound

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.175
impressive when you write. Once you say

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:45.360
the sentence out loud,

00:15:45.175 --> 00:15:47.840
>> [music]

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.000
>> then just write down exactly what you

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.000
said.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:53.800
You'll see that it'll keep your writing

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:56.200
style very conversational.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:58.720
Second, sleep on it.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:00.000
It's always good to come back to it the

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.640
next [music] day

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.920
